% ============================================================================
% cloud-type — each common cloud type and the weather it indicates
% (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% An ELEVENTH science slice, and a genuinely new axis from the already-shipped
% `meteorology/weather-instruments.adj` (instrument -> quantity it measures)
% and `oceanography/ocean-observing-instruments.adj` (instrument -> quantity)
% — this table does not name an instrument at all. It names a cloud TYPE and
% the weather condition its presence indicates:
% `cloud_type(cloud, weather_indication)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cloud_type(cirrus, $W)   % approaching_warm_front
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `cloud_type(cloud, weather_indication)` carrying the NWS citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% weather indication WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a cloud not one of the three the cited page names (e.g.
% altocumulus). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a weather indication and
% recall the cloud type that signals it:
%
%     ? cloud_type($C, heavy_rain_thunderstorm)   % cumulonimbus
%
% The three cloud types and the weather each one indicates, as a little
% truth table:
%
%     cloud          weather_indication            the cited quote
%     ------------   ---------------------------   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     cirrus         approaching_warm_front         "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level
%                                                     jet streak."
%     cumulonimbus   heavy_rain_thunderstorm        "a mature, deep cumulonimbus cloud, i.e., a thunderstorm producing heavy rain."
%     stratus        light_rain_drizzle_or_none     "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover
%                                                     which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation
%                                                     or drizzle."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from the National Weather Service's (Louisville
% forecast office) "Cloud Classification" education page. WebFetch-verified
% before writing (note: the related jetstream.noaa.gov domain 403s WebFetch
% entirely — weather.gov was used instead, per this stdlib's established
% workaround). `trust authoritative` — weather.gov is a primary U.S.
% government (NWS) science-education source, the same tier
% `weather-instruments.adj`'s and `precipitation-types.adj`'s NOAA/NWS
% sources earned. A cloud not one of these three — altocumulus, nimbostratus,
% cumulus — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a
% weather indication. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cloud_type {
    columns cloud, weather_indication

    row (cirrus, approaching_warm_front)
    row (cumulonimbus, heavy_rain_thunderstorm)
    row (stratus, light_rain_drizzle_or_none)

    source "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level jet streak."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
    trust authoritative
}
